---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can I put several photos in one PDF?', a: 'Yes. Select as many images as you need, or add more in several rounds. Use Move up and Move down to set the page order before you press Create PDF.' },
  { q: 'Are my images uploaded?', a: 'No. The PDF is built inside your browser using your own device. Nothing is sent to any server, so certificates and ID photos stay private.' },
  { q: 'Which image types work?', a: 'JPG, PNG and WebP images work in most browsers. Every image is converted to a JPEG page, and transparent areas become white.' },
  { q: 'Why is my PDF very large?', a: 'Phone photos are big. Choose "Smaller file" under image quality, or run the finished PDF through the compress PDF tool to hit a size limit.' },
  { q: 'Will phone photos come out sideways?', a: 'No. The tool reads the rotation stored in the photo, so pictures taken in portrait or landscape appear the right way up.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout slug="image-to-pdf" faqs={faqs}>
  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: lead paragraph, drafted by AI. */}
  <p slot="lead">
    This image to PDF converter joins your JPG, PNG or WebP pictures into a single PDF. Reorder the pages, choose A4
    or fit-to-image size, and download the file. It all happens in your browser, so nothing is uploaded.
  </p>

  <div slot="tool">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="ip-file">Choose images (you can pick several)</label>
      <input id="ip-file" type="file" accept="image/*" multiple />
    </div>
    <ul id="ip-list" class="thumbs" style="grid-template-columns:1fr"></ul>
    <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" id="ip-clear" class="btn secondary" hidden>Remove all</button></div>
    <div class="row three">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="ip-size">Page size</label>
        <select id="ip-size">
          <option value="a4-auto">A4, orientation follows each image</option>
          <option value="a4-p">A4 portrait</option>
          <option value="a4-l">A4 landscape</option>
          <option value="fit">Match each image</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="ip-margin">Margin</label>
        <select id="ip-margin"><option value="0">None</option><option value="18" selected>Small</option><option value="36">Large</option></select>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="ip-quality">Image quality</label>
        <select id="ip-quality"><option value="small">Smaller file</option><option value="balanced" selected>Balanced</option><option value="best">Best quality</option></select>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" id="ip-go" class="btn" disabled>Create PDF</button></div>
    <p id="ip-status" class="status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Choose one or more images to begin.</p>
    <div id="ip-result" class="result" hidden>
      <div id="ip-out" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" id="ip-dl" class="btn">Download PDF</button></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: how-to and worked example drafted by AI. */}
  <h2>How to turn images into a PDF</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Press the file button and choose your pictures. Add more any time.</li>
    <li>Check the list. Use Move up, Move down or Remove until the order is right.</li>
    <li>Choose the page size and margin. A4 suits printed documents; "Match each image" keeps every picture whole.</li>
    <li>Pick the image quality, press Create PDF, then download the result.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p class="worked">
    You have photos of the front and back of a certificate and your form wants one PDF. Add both pictures, put the
    front first, keep "A4, orientation follows each image" and leave quality on Balanced. You get a two-page PDF of
    roughly 600 KB. If the form allows only 300 KB, switch to "Smaller file" and create it again.
  </p>
  <h2>Tips for neat pages</h2>
  <p>
    Photograph documents flat, in daylight, with the page filling most of the frame. Crop away the table around the
    paper before you add the picture, because the tool places the whole image on the page. Check the final PDF before
    uploading, and follow the file rules in your official notification.
  </p>
  <script>import '../../scripts/image-to-pdf.js';</script>
</ToolLayout>
